WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.789
A mature header program starts with inventory and a baseline policy.

2
00:00:03.789 --> 00:00:12.217
Teams should know which headers are expected on HTML pages, APIs,
redirects, error pages, file downloads, static assets, and administrative
surfaces.

3
00:00:12.217 --> 00:00:19.927
Local and staging review should approximate production behavior closely
enough to catch missing or conflicting policies before release.

4
00:00:19.927 --> 00:00:23.128
Exceptions should be documented with owners and reasons.

5
00:00:23.128 --> 00:00:28.094
Testing should include regression checks for important headers on
important responses.

6
00:00:28.094 --> 00:00:37.894
These checks do not need to validate every browser behavior in detail, but
they should catch accidental removal, unsafe weakening, duplicate
conflicts, and environment drift.

7
00:00:37.894 --> 00:00:42.598
CSP report-only deployment can help teams discover policy gaps before
enforcement.

8
00:00:42.598 --> 00:00:49.523
Scanning and validation tools can support review, but they should not
replace understanding the application architecture.

9
00:00:49.523 --> 00:00:53.705
Monitoring should include the signals that matter for the chosen policies.

10
00:00:53.705 --> 00:01:01.087
CSP and browser security reports can identify blocked resources,
unexpected inline behavior, or broken third-party dependencies.

11
00:01:01.087 --> 00:01:08.340
Safe logging should avoid collecting secrets, tokens, or unnecessary user
data while preserving enough context to troubleshoot.

12
00:01:08.340 --> 00:01:14.873
Teams should review reports after frontend changes, dependency updates,
domain changes, or deployment migrations.

13
00:01:14.873 --> 00:01:17.944
Header policies should evolve as applications evolve.

14
00:01:17.944 --> 00:01:26.306
New APIs, frontend bundles, embedded widgets, partner resources, domains,
static hosting changes, and proxy changes can all affect header behavior.

15
00:01:26.306 --> 00:01:31.795
Change management should include header review for releases that alter
browser-facing behavior.

16
00:01:31.795 --> 00:01:40.680
The goal is maintainable defense in depth: clear defaults, controlled
exceptions, observable behavior, and periodic review rather than a one-
time checklist.
